Since there is only one possible value of <imath>AD</imath>, we assume <imath>\angle{B}=90^{\circ}</imath>. By the angle bisector theorem, <imath>\frac{AB}{AC}=\frac{2}{3}</imath>, so <imath>AB=2\sqrt{5}</imath> and <imath>AC=3\sqrt{5}</imath>. Now observe that <imath>\angle{BAD}=90^{\circ}</imath>. Let the intersection of <imath>BD</imath> and <imath>AP</imath> be <imath>X</imath>. Then <imath>\angle{ABD}=90^{\circ}-\angle{BAX}=\angle{APB}</imath>. Consequently, <cmath>\bigtriangleup DAB \sim \bigtriangleup ABP</cmath> and therefore <imath>\frac{DA}{AB} = \frac{AB}{BP}</imath>, so <imath>AD=\boxed{\textbf{(C) }10}</imath>, and we're done!

